                                 RAM* HELP
===============================================================================
To print this help file: in DOS, type "PRINT RAM.HLP"
(Your printout will contain many "%" and "!"; they are just markers to
 highlight specific characters.)
===============================================================================

Each of the following examples describes a typical procedure for
a particular basic task.  (Refer to the summaries of MAIN-MENU
and RUN/EDIT that appear below the examples for explanations.)

To enter a new program and run it:
   (a) Select "%!Run/Edit" from MAIN-MENU.
   (b) Select "Edit%!Memory"; then enter and edit your program.
   (c) From MAIN-MENU, select "%!Save memory".
   (d) If you wish to change the default initial PC (Program Counter), then
       from MAIN-MENU, select "%!PC setting".
   (e) If you wish to use a diskfile for RAM* input (instead of the default
       KEYBOARD input), then from MAIN-MENU, select "%Input from"; then choose
       a RAM* filename from those listed on the bottom half of the screen.
   (f) From MAIN-MENU select "%!Run/Edit".
   (g) Select "%!Step-by-step" or "%!Continuous" to run your program.
   (h) If you are not satisfied with the results, then edit your program.
   (i) If you wish to restart your program, then go back to MAIN-MENU; then
       select "%!Tools"; then select "%!Reset" and return to step (g).
   (j) When you are finished or just wish to quit:  remember to SAVE your
       program before you leave RAM* if you do not wish to lose it!

To load a previously saved program and run it:
   (a) In MAIN-MENU the stored programs for RAM* are listed at the bottom half
       of the screen.  Select "%!Load program" and choose one of those
       filenames.
   (b) Go to step (d) of the previous example.
   [ Suggestion: Try loading and running ADD.RAM
     (See comments and explanations by invoking %!EditComt.) ]


                            SUMMARY OF MAIN-MENU
                            --------------------
%!Load program
   After you choose a valid filename, RAM* will clear all registers, the
   memory, and the input and output lists.  Then it will load the program of
   that filename into memory, and set the PC.

%!Run/Edit
   (See "SUMMARY OF RUN/EDIT" section below.)

%!Save memory
   Save the current PC setting and the non-hardwired memory -- cells 01-98.

%!Input from
   This selection will toggle between KEYBOARD and a filename.  If it is
   KEYBOARD, then during execution each read-input instruction will request
   input from the keyboard; otherwise it will take input from the specified
   file.

   Any file saved by RAM* can be used as input, since input is just a stream
   of "numbers".  When a program file is used as input, the first input item
   is the content of address 1 in memory at the time the file was saved.  The
   subsequent input items to be read in are the contents of memory occuring
   sequentially after address 1 when the file was saved.  The last input item
   is the last non-"+000".  All the trailing "+000" are ignored, and are not
   included in the input stream.

%!PC setting
   Enter the initial PC (Program Counter) value, i.e., the cell number of the
   first program instruction to be executed.

%!Clear Everything
   Clear every component of RAM*.

%!File Maintenance
   To rename, delete, or copy RAM* files.

%!Help
   Display the help file you are now reading.

%!Quit
   Exit from RAM*.


                            SUMMARY OF RUN/EDIT
                            -------------------
When you select "%!Run/Edit" from the MAIN-MENU, the RUN/EDIT screen will
replace the MAIN-MENU screen.  On the right half of the screen, the memory
is labeled with horizontal and vertical coordinates.  (To find the address of
a cell, add the horizontal and vertical coordinates of that cell.)  The blank
memory cells represent "+000" contents.  Three registers (ACC, IR, and PC)
appear on the left side of the screen, with two scrolling Input/Output list
display windows below them.  The rectangle just above the three registers
describes the next operation to be performed.  A menu line appears at the
bottom of the screen:

%!<%!E%!S%!C%!>=exit
   Return to MAIN-MENU.  (This does not erase the current state of execution.
   If you select "%!Run/Edit" from MAIN-MENU again, you will see that the state
   of every component has been preserved, unless you have changed one of the
   other settings on MAIN-MENU.)

%!Help
   Display the help file you are now reading.  (Same as %!Help in MAIN-MENU.)

%!Tools
   A new menu line appears at the bottom of the screen:
   %!Reset
      Clear all registers, then reload the program.  And clear the input and
      output lists.  (If the input is from a diskfile, then reload that file
      into the input.)  If you have not yet saved the program after modifying
      the memory contents with the editor, then save it from the MAIN-MENU
      before resetting.
   Change-Setting: %!AC %!IR %!PC
      After selecting one of the three registers, enter its new contents.
   %!Sound=YES/NO
      Default = YES.  When NO, the "click" after each operation is not
      generated.

%!Step-by-step
   In one machine cycle, FETCH and EXECUTE operations are performed: an
   instruction is copied from memory to the IR (Instruction Register), and the
   PC is incremented; then the instruction is carried out.  In step-by-step
   execution mode, at each spacebar press, half of the machine cycle will
   complete (either FETCH or EXECUTE).  The cycle continues (FETCH and EXECUTE
   operations alternate) until one of the following four interruptions occurs:
   - When you press the ESC key at anytime, the next fetch or execution
     is suspended.  (It can always be resumed when you select %!Step-by-step
     again.)
   - When executing the "input" instruction with input from the keyboard, the
     cursor will blink at the bottom of the input list window, waiting for
     you to enter an item.  (ESC key can be pressed here too to suspend
     entering an input item.)
   - When an error is reported.
   - When the "halt" instruction is executed.

%!Continuous
   This execution mode is just like the step-by-step mode, except that it does
   not wait for you to press the spacebar after each FETCH and EXECUTE
   operation; the FETCH-EXECUTE cycle continues until one of the interruptions
   described above occurs.

Edit%!Memory
   At the left side of the screen, the Edit Help window displaying all the
   instruction codes and their descriptions appears, and each of the cells
   that were blank now shows '+000'.  Use the arrow keys to move the cursor
   around the memory (the SPACEBAR and BACKSPACE keys have the same effect
   as right and left arrow keys, respectively).  Each cell of the memory
   contains four fields: a sign (+ or -) field followed by three Arabic digit
   fields.  A RETURN key press will move the cursor to the first digit field
   of the next cell (useful when typing in a program).  To modify a memory
   cell's content, move the cursor to the desired location;  then type a '-'
   or '+' if the cursor is on the sign field, and a number if the cursor is
   on the digit field.  Press <ESC> to exit the editor.

   RAM* instructions are three digits %!O%!A%!A: the first an operation code,
   the next two a memory cell address (except for the shift instruction.)
   RAM* op codes:

          I   Input/Output                   ----------
                     %!0  Input   ---------->  | Memory |
                     %!1  Output <----------   |________|

          II  Arithmetic
    ---------------  %!2 Clear and Load Accumulator <------  ----------
    | Accumulator |  %!3 Store Accumltr in memory   ------>  | Memory |
    ---------------  %!4 Add to Accumulator         <------  |        |
                     %!5 Subtract from Accumulator  <------  ----------

          III Control of Program Counter Register
                     %!7 Jump to the given memory cell  |    --------
                     %!8 Test Accum. - jump if negative |--> |  PC  |
                     %!9 Reset to xx and Halt           |    --------

          IV  Shift accumulator
                       -----------this many places left,
                       | ---------then this many places right.
                       | |                              ---------------
                     %!6 _ _                       -----> | Accumulator |
                                                        ---------------

%!EditComt
   This allows you to edit memory contents plus a line of comments for each
   address.  Comments can be any pattern of characters you can type.  Choose
   %!Help to get the Edit Help window.

   Commenting code: always state the basic function performed by the program,
   and specify the correct input format and outputs.  Comment area can also
   be used to draw brackets to identify submodules of the code, and to draw
   arrows to show flow of control.


For an introduction to random-access von Neumann-architecture machines, see
chapters 15 and 45 in A. K. Dewdney, THE TURING OMNIBUS (W. H. Freeman, NY,
1989).  [chs 17 & 48, 1993 ed]  

